# The root filesystem the kernel mounts -- what `b6fsutil -n -M' builds into root.img,
# which `-S' then converts into the SIMH container kernel/test/CMakeLists.txt attaches as
# md00.  Grammar: cmd/fsutil/README.md.
#
# The tree, the device nodes, and the USERLAND -- /etc/init, /bin/sh, the five commands that
# prove the prompt, the ten of the file-management set and the eight small utilities of task
# C2, plus the static files of /etc.
# Task 24 put them here and task 25b
# flipped the last line, /etc/init, from the task-23 stand-in to the real one.  Task 25c
# added /usr/test, the lib/test programs that b6sim also runs; the termcap port added
# /etc/termcap and termcapt beside them, which are lib/libtermcap's rather than libc's, and
# the curses port added cursest and curstty, which are lib/libcurses' and read the same file.
# Task C1a (cmd/README.md) added /bin/mkdir and /bin/rmdir, which are the first entries here
# to carry a mode the staging tree cannot express: they are SETUID ROOT.  Task C1b added
# /bin/{cp,ln,mv,rm} and a third setuid program in mv, and task C1c added
# /bin/{chgrp,chmod,chown,touch} -- the four that change an inode rather than a directory,
# and the four that finished the file-management set.  None of THOSE is setuid: chmod(2) is
# gated on owner(), which admits the file's owner, and chown(2) on suser(), which is the rule
# that stops a user giving a file away.  Task C2a added /bin/{date,kill,sleep} -- the clock, a
# signal and an alarm -- and task C2b /bin/{basename,test,time,tty,yes}, none of them setuid
# either, together with THE FIRST HARD LINK this file has ever carried: /bin/[ is /bin/test.
# Kernel task 29b added the three entries that make this a MULTI-USER image: /etc/getty,
# /bin/login and the /etc/ttys that sends init looking for them -- plus /usr/guest, the first
# directory here that is not root's, and /usr/test/ttyt, which asserts that ttyslot(3) can now
# find a line in that file.
#
# WHY THIS FILE LIVES AT THE TOP OF THE TREE and not in kernel/test/ beside the CMakeLists
# that runs b6fsutil over it: every `file' below but none of the directories comes out of
# build/rootfs/, which is staged by cmd/init, cmd/sh, cmd/getty, cmd/login,
# cmd/{basename,cat,chgrp,chmod,chown,cp,date,echo,kill,ln,ls,mkdir,mv,pwd,rm,rmdir,sleep,
# sync,test,time,touch,tty,yes}, lib/test and etc/.  The one exception is /bin/[, which comes out of no directory at all: it is a `link'
# stanza and its inode is /bin/test's.
# The image is the whole project's, and it is the top-level CMakeLists.txt that draws the
# `rootimg depends on rootfs' edge, since kernel/ is configured before rootfs exists.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT MOVE WITH IT is b6fsutil's working directory: it is still the kernel test
# BUILD directory, because that is where root.img and root3072.disk have to land for the
# .ini files to attach them.  A `source' is resolved against THE PROCESS'S CWD, not against
# this file's own location, so moving the manifest changed no path below.  From
# build/kernel/test/, `../../rootfs' is build/rootfs/, and that holds for any build
# directory, since both ends are under CMAKE_BINARY_DIR.
#
# CONSEQUENCE: this manifest depends on the top-level project.  A standalone
# `cmake -S kernel' can still build and boot the kernel, but it cannot build root.img,
# because nothing there stages a build/rootfs/ for these paths to point at.
#
# NOTHING WRITES THIS IMAGE IN PLACE.  root.img and root3072.disk are build artifacts, and
# the one test that needs a writable filesystem -- kernel/test/session, which creates files,
# runs sync(2) and then fscks what reached the disk -- converts its own copy at its own
# volume number first (kernel/test/run-session.sh).  The same trick, `b6fsutil -a', is how
# that test gets its script onto the image without a line here, and how coninit can be put
# back as /etc/init by hand when a console failure needs bisecting against a program that
# links neither crt0 nor libc.
#
# Three tests read this image: kernel/test/fstest, which checks the superblock and the root
# inode below the boot path, and kernel/test/boot + kernel/test/console, which boot a
# kernel against it.  kernel/test/login boots a copy too and is the one that WRITES the most
# of it -- /etc/utmp made and filled in, and a device node's owner changed by login(1) -- which
# is why run-login.sh fscks what comes back and diffs four inodes out of `b6fsutil -v -v'.
#
# THE DEVICE NUMBERS ARE kernel/conf.c's, and that file is the authority: the major is the
# row index in bdevsw[] (0 md, 1 mb) or cdevsw[] (0 console, 1 mem, 2 tty, 3 rmd, 4 rmb).
# A node here that names a row conf.c does not have opens onto nothing.  Major 0 is the
# only one with more than one minor that matters: dev/sc.c drives BOTH Consul typewriters,
# minor 0 being the operator's and minor 1 the second terminal.
#
# For the disk, the MINOR IS THE SIMULATOR'S UNIT SUBSCRIPT -- bit 5 the controller, bits
# 4-3 the group (линейка), bits 2-0 the drive -- so /dev/md0's minor 0 is SIMH's md00, and
# a minor number and an `attach' line are the same number.  There are no partitions: one
# EC-5052 drive is 2000 blocks and swap lives on the drums (/dev/swap, block major 1).
#
# SINCE TASK C4c THERE ARE TWO DRIVES' WORTH OF NODES, md0/rmd0 and md1/rmd1, and only the
# first is attached by an ordinary boot: kernel/test/mkfs is the one test that hangs a
# second pack on md01, and the raw node is what /etc/mkfs writes a filesystem onto.  A node
# for a drive nobody attached is not an error -- mdopen() accepts any minor below 64 and the
# refusal comes at the first transfer -- so these two cost nothing on every other boot.
#
# AND SINCE NMOUNT WENT FROM 2 TO 8 THERE ARE EIGHT, md0 through md7.  The kernel can hold
# eight mounts and iinit() takes one for the root, so seven filesystems can be on this
# machine at once; without the nodes to name them that is capacity nothing can reach, and
# `mknod' on a running system does not survive the image being rebuilt.  md3-md7 carry no
# raw node, for md2's reason: nothing has ever wanted the raw form of a drive that exists
# only to be mounted, and /dev/rmdN is the whole of a pack to whoever can open it.

default
owner 0
group 0
dirmode 0755
filemode 0644

dir /bin
dir /dev
dir /etc
dir /usr
dir /usr/test

# diff -h's other half, task C5f.  THE FIRST ENTRY THIS IMAGE HAS EVER HAD IN /usr/lib, and
# the only program of that task that is not in /bin: diff(1) reaches it with
# execv("/usr/lib/diffh", args), so the path here and the string in cmd/diff/diff.c are one
# and have to stay so.  It is diff's way out of memory -- diff holds both files in core and
# says `files too big, try -h' when they will not fit, which on a 28,672-word address space
# is nearer than it was on a PDP-11 (cmd/diff/README.md).
dir /usr/lib

# look(1)'s default dictionary.  v7's /usr/dict/words is not in this tree -- the
# reference copy under cmd/tmp/ is git-ignored -- so cmd/look/words is a small sorted
# list written for the purpose, staged by cmd/look/CMakeLists.txt.  It is what makes the
# bare `look word' form mean anything here, and it is the ONE assertion about look that
# b6sim cannot make: under the simulator this path is the build machine's (SS9), so every
# case in cmd/look/test names its dictionary explicitly and kernel/test/filters is where
# the default is exercised.
dir /usr/dict

dir /tmp
mode 0777

# fsck(1M)'s reconnect target, task C4d.  An orphaned inode -- allocated, with nothing in
# any directory pointing at it -- is put here under its i-number, and fsck can only do that
# if the directory ALREADY EXISTS: it has no way to make one, having no free-list
# discipline of its own to allocate with.
#
# AN EMPTY ONE IS ENOUGH, which fsck.1m denies.  That page says to create files in it and
# delete them again so that empty slots exist; here linkup() rounds the directory's size up
# to a whole block, a block holds DIRPB (128) entries, and everything past `.' and `..' is
# zeros -- alloc() clears the buffer it hands out (kernel/alloc.c) and b6fsutil's dir.cpp
# starts from a zero Block.  So this has 126 free slots the moment it is made.  cmd/fsck's
# manual page carries the correction.
dir /lost+found

# mount(1M)'s target, task C4f.  It has to exist and it has to be EMPTY of anything anybody
# wants: mounting covers it, and whatever is underneath is unreachable until the unmount.
# It also has to be nobody's working directory when the mount happens -- smount() refuses a
# mount point whose inode is held more than once (i_count != 1, kernel/sys3.c) with EBUSY.
dir /mnt

# guest's home, and the first directory here that is not root's.  login(1) chdir()s to the
# pw_dir field of /etc/passwd and prints `No directory' if it cannot, so the account is only
# usable because this exists -- and being uid 7's is what lets a non-root shell write in it.
dir /usr/guest
owner 7
group 3

# ---- the program the icode execs, and the shell it will exec in turn -------------

file /etc/init          # cmd/init: the v7 /etc/init
source ../../rootfs/etc/init
mode 0755

# /etc/getty and NOT /bin/getty: cmd/init/init.c's `char getty[] = "/etc/getty"' is the path
# init execs, and nothing looks anywhere else.  It is the second program of task 29b, and the
# one that makes /etc/ttys below mean something.  0755 and not setuid -- init is root and this
# is its child.
file /etc/getty         # cmd/getty: the login: prompt, one per enabled line of /etc/ttys
source ../../rootfs/etc/getty
mode 0755

# /etc AND NOT /bin, which is v7's placement and the reason the two `ls /bin' expectations
# in kernel/test did not have to grow for this one.  quot is section 1M: it sweeps the whole
# i-list off /dev/rmd0, so it needs root and is of no use to anybody else.  NOT setuid, for
# the reason df is not -- see /bin/df above and cmd/df/README.md.
file /etc/quot          # cmd/quot -- blocks per uid, by sweeping the i-list off /dev/rmd0
source ../../rootfs/etc/quot
mode 0755

# quot's placement, and quot's reasons: section 1M, root-only, NOT setuid.  This one is
# sharper than the other two, though -- mkfs WRITES the raw device, and it is the first
# program on this image that does.  A setuid mkfs would let anybody destroy every file on
# the volume with one command.  cmd/mkfs/README.md is the account of that write path.
file /etc/mkfs          # cmd/mkfs -- make a filesystem, on /dev/rmd1 and nothing else here
source ../../rootfs/etc/mkfs
mode 0755

# quot's and mkfs's placement again, and the sharpest case of their reason: this one WRITES
# other people's files.  /dev/rmd0 is mode 0600 because that node is every file's contents,
# and a setuid fsck would hand the whole volume to anyone who could think of an i-number.
# Not setuid; root-only; section 1M.
file /etc/fsck          # cmd/fsck -- check a filesystem, and repair it
source ../../rootfs/etc/fsck
mode 0755

# The four pre-fsck tools, task C4e -- each doing one of fsck's jobs standalone.  fsck's
# placement and fsck's reasons, and two of them are the sharp case: clri WRITES an i-node
# and `icheck -s' rewrites the free list of the whole volume, so a setuid one of either
# would hand every file on the disk to anybody who could think of an i-number.  None of the
# four is setuid; all four are root-only, and all four are section 1M.  Being in /etc is
# also why the two hard-coded `ls /bin' expectations in kernel/test did not have to grow.
file /etc/clri          # cmd/clri -- zero an i-node that appears in no directory
source ../../rootfs/etc/clri
mode 0755

file /etc/dcheck        # cmd/dcheck -- directory entries against link counts
source ../../rootfs/etc/dcheck
mode 0755

file /etc/icheck        # cmd/icheck -- block accounting, and -s lays the free list down again
source ../../rootfs/etc/icheck
mode 0755

file /etc/ncheck        # cmd/ncheck -- i-number to path name; `ncheck | sort | quot -n'
source ../../rootfs/etc/ncheck
mode 0755

# The pair of task C4f, and the one that finishes C4: they are what puts a second filesystem
# behind /mnt below.  Section 1M and root-only for a reason that is not the other eight's --
# these two do not touch the raw device at all.  What gates them is /dev/md1, mode 0600 and
# root's, which is where mount(2) takes its privilege from: it asks for none of its own, so a
# setuid mount would let anybody graft a filesystem of their own making -- setuid-root files
# and all -- onto this one.  cmd/mount/README.md SS4.
file /etc/mount         # cmd/mount -- mount a filesystem, on /dev/md1 through the buffer cache
source ../../rootfs/etc/mount
mode 0755

file /etc/umount        # cmd/umount -- and take it away again
source ../../rootfs/etc/umount
mode 0755

# /etc AND NOT /bin, which is v7's arrangement: wall's section is 1M and cmd/sh's default path
# reaches /bin only, so it is typed with its path.  NOT setuid, and its own manual page is the
# argument -- "the sender should be super-user to override any protections the users may have
# invoked" is a statement about the ordinary permission check on a 0600 terminal, and a setuid
# wall would hand that override to everybody.  cmd/write is the opposite case and is 0755 too,
# for the opposite reason: it makes the mesg(1) check itself.
file /etc/wall          # cmd/wall -- task C6.  One forked child per live /etc/utmp record
source ../../rootfs/etc/wall
mode 0755

# /etc/mtab IS NOT HERE, and that is the design rather than an omission: the first mount of a
# boot creates it (cmd/mount/mtab.c), a missing one reads as an empty table, and an image
# that shipped one would be shipping a claim about what is mounted that no boot had made.

file /bin/sh            # cmd/sh: S. R. Bourne's shell
source ../../rootfs/bin/sh
mode 0755

# ---- enough commands to prove the prompt -----------------------------------------

file /bin/basename      # cmd/basename
source ../../rootfs/bin/basename
mode 0755

file /bin/cal           # cmd/cal -- task C5f.  September 1752 still loses eleven days.
source ../../rootfs/bin/cal
mode 0755

file /bin/cat           # cmd/cat -- and what /etc/rc uses to print the motd
source ../../rootfs/bin/cat
mode 0755

file /bin/chgrp         # cmd/chgrp -- reads /etc/group; NOT setuid, chown(2) is suser()-gated
source ../../rootfs/bin/chgrp
mode 0755

file /bin/chmod         # cmd/chmod -- NOT setuid: chmod(2) is gated on owner(), not suser()
source ../../rootfs/bin/chmod
mode 0755

file /bin/chown         # cmd/chown -- reads /etc/passwd; NOT setuid, chown(2) is suser()-gated
source ../../rootfs/bin/chown
mode 0755

# TASK C5a's SIX -- cmp, rev, split, sum, tee and wc -- are NOT a block here.  This list is
# alphabetical and they fall where their names do, so each carries its own `task C5a' note
# instead: cmp below, then rev, split, sum, tee and wc further down.  They are the first of
# the text filters, the phase that makes a userland testable -- almost every case they carry
# runs under b6sim in a tenth of a second, with no boot at all (cmd/README.md SS9) -- and not
# one of the six is setuid, each opening, creating or reading exactly what the caller could
# itself.

file /bin/cmp           # cmd/cmp -- task C5a; the seven `long's cmd/README.md SS3 counted
source ../../rootfs/bin/cmp
mode 0755

file /bin/col           # cmd/col -- task C5b, and the task's one deliberate divergence: v7's
                        # Greek half-shift stole bit 0200 of every character and its input
                        # mask deleted the rest, so no UTF-8 letter survived.  Both are gone.
source ../../rootfs/bin/col
mode 0755

file /bin/comm          # cmd/comm -- task C5b.  Its collating order is this machine's byte
                        # order: char is unsigned here, so a byte above 0177 sorts AFTER the
                        # ASCII where v7's signed char put it before (SS3).
source ../../rootfs/bin/comm
mode 0755

file /bin/cp            # cmd/cp -- and what cmd/mv execs for a move across devices
source ../../rootfs/bin/cp
mode 0755

file /bin/date          # cmd/date -- what /etc/rc prints after the motd; NOT setuid, stime(2)
                        # being suser()-gated, and that is the point
source ../../rootfs/bin/date
mode 0755

# Task C4b, and the reason it is here rather than in /etc beside quot: dd is a general copy
# program that happens to be the only way to reach a device in bulk, not a device tool.  NOT
# setuid, and least of all this one -- /dev/rmd0 is mode 0600 because that node is every
# file's contents, and a setuid dd would let anybody rewrite them.  cmd/dd/dd.c is the
# account of the four alignment rules it obeys and of the 3072-byte block it defaults to.
file /bin/dd            # cmd/dd -- copy and convert, and the only bulk path to /dev/rmd0
source ../../rootfs/bin/dd
mode 0755

# The two of task C4a that live in /bin.  Both report in 1024-byte blocks -- three per
# filesystem block, so their numbers are half a PDP-11's and always a multiple of three;
# /etc/quot is the third and is further down, being root-only.
# df is NOT setuid and must not become one -- it reads /dev/rmd0, which is mode 0600 because
# that one node is every file's contents -- so it works for root and nobody else.  du opens
# no device at all and works for anybody.  cmd/df/README.md is the account of the four
# alignment rules a raw read here obeys.
file /bin/df            # cmd/df -- free blocks, by walking the free list off /dev/rmd0
source ../../rootfs/bin/df
mode 0755

file /bin/diff          # cmd/diff -- task C5f.  Hunt-Szymanski over Stone's k-candidates,
                        # so a shortest edit script but not the same one GNU diff picks; what
                        # cmd/diff/test asserts is that the -e script really does turn file1
                        # into file2.  -b's blank set is written out rather than taken from
                        # ctype(3), whose table stops at 0177.
source ../../rootfs/bin/diff
mode 0755

file /bin/du            # cmd/du -- blocks per directory, by stat(2) and a directory walk
source ../../rootfs/bin/du
mode 0755

file /bin/echo          # cmd/echo
source ../../rootfs/bin/echo
mode 0755

file /bin/ed            # cmd/ed -- task C3, and THE FIRST PROGRAM ON THIS IMAGE THAT WRITES
                        # TO /tmp: its temp file is /tmp/e<pid><letter>, in 512-byte blocks
                        # of ed's own (cmd/README.md SS4), unlinked on a clean quit.
source ../../rootfs/bin/ed
mode 0755

file /bin/file          # cmd/file -- task C5f, and the one deliberate change of it: the
                        # magic numbers are this machine's (FMAGIC/NMAGIC/ARMAG), RELFLG
                        # tells a linked executable from a relocatable object, the assembler
                        # tables are b6as's -- and WELL-FORMED UTF-8 IS TEXT, where v7 called
                        # every byte above 0177 `data' and so reported this image's own
                        # Russian prose as binary.  A malformed byte is still data.
source ../../rootfs/bin/file
mode 0755

file /bin/fgrep         # cmd/fgrep -- task C5c, with grep below.  MAXSIZ is 3000 states and
                        # not v7's 6000: a `struct words' is four words here, so the v7
                        # table alone was 24,000 of the 28,672 words the address space has.
source ../../rootfs/bin/fgrep
mode 0755

file /bin/find          # cmd/find -- task C5f, and the only program of it with no b6sim
                        # test at all: it reads directories, popen()s pwd and fork/execs.
                        # -size is in 1024-byte blocks, as df/du/quot/ls -s are, where v7's
                        # were 512; -cpio is gone (a PDP-11 tape archive with a prompt for
                        # the next reel); and the directory walk is bounded at 20 levels,
                        # v7 having recursed with its block in the frame and checked nothing.
source ../../rootfs/bin/find
mode 0755

file /bin/grep          # cmd/grep -- task C5c.  A character class is 256 bits rather than
                        # v7's 128, so a byte above 0177 has a bit of its own; that closed a
                        # store past the class as well.  -b prints a BYTE OFFSET, which is a
                        # deliberate divergence: v7's block number was a PDP-11 512-byte
                        # disk block here and grep and fgrep disagreed about the same match.
source ../../rootfs/bin/grep
mode 0755

file /bin/join          # cmd/join -- task C5f.  A line holds at most 20 fields and 3071
                        # bytes, both of which v7 stated nowhere and enforced nowhere; a
                        # join field past the end of a line is the empty string rather than
                        # a null pointer; and file2 may not be `-', the many-to-many path
                        # rewinding it with fseek.
source ../../rootfs/bin/join
mode 0755

file /bin/kill          # cmd/kill -- NOT setuid: kill(2) matches uids for anyone but root
source ../../rootfs/bin/kill
mode 0755

file /bin/ln            # cmd/ln
source ../../rootfs/bin/ln
mode 0755

# NOT SETUID, and it is the entry on this list where that is worth a second look, because
# login is the one program here that really does change uid: /etc/getty execs it as root and
# setuid() hands the terminal to whoever answered the prompt.  The privilege is INHERITED, not
# borrowed -- so 0755, as the v7 tree ships it.  Making it setuid would let any user re-run it
# from a shell, which is cmd/su's job (task C6).
file /bin/login         # cmd/login: crypt(3), and the first non-root shell on this machine
source ../../rootfs/bin/login
mode 0755

file /bin/look          # cmd/look -- task C5b.  Its default dictionary is /usr/dict/words
                        # above; -d treats a byte over 0177 as a word constituent and -f
                        # leaves it alone, a multi-byte letter having no case (SS11).
source ../../rootfs/bin/look
mode 0755

file /bin/ls            # cmd/ls
source ../../rootfs/bin/ls
mode 0755

file /bin/mesg          # cmd/mesg -- task C6.  NOT setuid: chmod(2) is gated on owner(), and
                        # the terminal is the user's only because login chowned it.  The nodes
                        # below ship 0622, so a fresh boot answers `is y'.
source ../../rootfs/bin/mesg
mode 0755

file /bin/novi          # cmd/novi -- Dave W Plummer's full-screen editor; task C12, and the
                        # first program here that is not a port of a v7 command.  Its document
                        # is never in memory: two unlinked /tmp files hold it with a gap at
                        # the cursor,
                        # and a third is the cut buffer.  NOT setuid -- it reads and writes
                        # as its caller.  Wants an ANSI terminal; the Consul is one only
                        # because SIMH's line is raw8 and passes the escapes through.
source ../../rootfs/bin/novi
mode 0755

file /bin/od            # cmd/od -- task C5b.  -o/-d/-x go on meaning THE MACHINE WORD, which
                        # is 48 bits here: 16 octal digits, 15 decimal, 12 hex.  -w is a
                        # second name for -o.  Bytes pack most significant first, as getw(3).
source ../../rootfs/bin/od
mode 0755

file /bin/pr            # cmd/pr -- task C5f.  Its multi-column look-ahead lives in one
                        # 6720-byte ring, and a page that needs more of it than that is a
                        # diagnostic: v7 checked nothing there and silently began the
                        # affected column part way down the file.  The two in-band sentinels
                        # 0375 and 0376 are kept, neither byte being reachable in UTF-8.
                        # Columns are counted in bytes, as col(1)'s are.
source ../../rootfs/bin/pr
mode 0755

file /bin/pwd           # cmd/pwd
source ../../rootfs/bin/pwd
mode 0755

file /bin/rev           # cmd/rev -- task C5a, and the one deliberate divergence in it: it
                        # reverses UTF-8 SEQUENCES where v7 reversed bytes, this machine's
                        # text being UTF-8 since task C11.  See cmd/rev/README.md.
source ../../rootfs/bin/rev
mode 0755

file /bin/rm            # cmd/rm -- NOT setuid: `rm -r' execs /bin/rmdir, which is
source ../../rootfs/bin/rm
mode 0755

file /bin/sed           # cmd/sed -- task C5e.  A character class is 256 bits and a y///
                        # table 256 entries, where v7's were 128 of each and each was
                        # indexed by an UNMASKED byte on one side: the first stored past
                        # the class it was compiling and the second read past the table it
                        # was applying.  A backslash-escaped byte of an s/// replacement is
                        # marked with a QESC prefix rather than with bit 0200 of the byte,
                        # so `s/x/привет/' produces привет and not ten bytes of plausible
                        # ASCII.  A line past 4000 bytes is a diagnostic on the input path
                        # and on the output path alike; v7 truncated one in silence and
                        # announced the other without acting on it.
source ../../rootfs/bin/sed
mode 0755

file /bin/sleep         # cmd/sleep -- a decimal parse around libc's sleep(3): alarm + pause
source ../../rootfs/bin/sleep
mode 0755

file /bin/sort          # cmd/sort -- task C5d.  Its temp files are /tmp/stm<pid><letters>,
                        # made and unlinked as it runs: /usr/tmp is not tried, this image
                        # having none.  -d and -i keep a byte above 0177 SIGNIFICANT, where
                        # v7's tables ignored all of 0200..0377 and so made two Cyrillic
                        # words compare equal.  A line longer than 3072 bytes is a diagnostic
                        # on both the sort and the merge path; v7 truncated it on one of them
                        # and had no limit at all on the other.
source ../../rootfs/bin/sort
mode 0755

file /bin/split         # cmd/split -- task C5a; the pieces land in the working directory
source ../../rootfs/bin/split
mode 0755

file /bin/stty          # cmd/stty -- task C6.  NOT setuid: gtty/stty are gated on holding
                        # the descriptor.  Its capability list is CUT to what dev/tty.c
                        # honours -- no speeds, no parity, no delays, no LCASE, no hup.
source ../../rootfs/bin/stty
mode 0755

file /bin/sum           # cmd/sum -- task C5a.  Its block count is in 1024-byte blocks, like
                        # df's, du's, quot's and `ls -s' (cmd/README.md SS4); v7's was in the
                        # PDP-11's 512-byte ones.  The checksum is bit for bit v7's.
source ../../rootfs/bin/sum
mode 0755

file /bin/sync          # cmd/sync -- what flushes the buffer cache before the machine stops
source ../../rootfs/bin/sync
mode 0755

file /bin/tee           # cmd/tee -- task C5a, and the only one of the six with no stdio at
                        # all: read(2) and write(2), in BSIZE buffers where v7 used the
                        # PDP-11's 512
source ../../rootfs/bin/tee
mode 0755

file /bin/tail          # cmd/tail -- task C5b.  `-b' counts BSIZE here, not the PDP-11's 512
                        # (SS4), so it agrees with dd's `b' suffix.  No stdio at all.
source ../../rootfs/bin/tail
mode 0755

file /bin/test          # cmd/test -- what lets a shell script branch; see /bin/[ below
source ../../rootfs/bin/test
mode 0755

file /bin/time          # cmd/time -- times(2) in 250ths of a second here, not the PDP-11's 60ths
source ../../rootfs/bin/time
mode 0755

file /bin/touch         # cmd/touch -- utime(2) here, not v7's rewrite-a-byte; see cmd/touch
source ../../rootfs/bin/touch
mode 0755

file /bin/tr            # cmd/tr -- task C5b.  The one filter of the seven that was eight-bit
                        # clean already: its tables are [256] as v7 wrote them, so `tr a-z
                        # A-Z' folds the ASCII and leaves a Cyrillic letter alone (SS11).
source ../../rootfs/bin/tr
mode 0755

file /bin/tsort         # cmd/tsort -- task C5f, and lorder(1)'s other half.  An item is
                        # any run of bytes with no blank in it, so a Cyrillic name sorts
                        # like any other; one past 255 bytes -- ar(1)'s longest member
                        # name here -- is a diagnostic where v7 overran a 50-byte buffer.
source ../../rootfs/bin/tsort
mode 0755

file /bin/tty           # cmd/tty -- ttyname(3), which reads /dev as a directory
source ../../rootfs/bin/tty
mode 0755

file /bin/uniq          # cmd/uniq -- task C5b.  Compares whole bytes and masks none, so a
                        # Cyrillic line compares like any other (SS11).
source ../../rootfs/bin/uniq
mode 0755

file /bin/wc            # cmd/wc -- task C5a.  A byte above 0177 is a WORD CONSTITUENT here,
                        # not a delimiter as v7 had it, so a Cyrillic line counts (SS11); the
                        # characters it counts are bytes.
source ../../rootfs/bin/wc
mode 0755

file /bin/who           # cmd/who -- task C6.  The first reader /etc/utmp has ever had; init
                        # and login were the only programs that touched that file before it.
source ../../rootfs/bin/who
mode 0755

file /bin/write         # cmd/write -- task C6.  NOT setuid: the terminal nodes are 0622, so
                        # the open needs nothing -- and write makes the mesg(1) check ITSELF,
                        # which a setuid bit would be defeating rather than enabling.
source ../../rootfs/bin/write
mode 0755

file /bin/yes           # cmd/yes -- the only program here that a SIGPIPE has to stop
source ../../rootfs/bin/yes
mode 0755

# ---- and the one file that is a second NAME rather than a second file -------------
#
# THE ONLY HARD LINK ON THIS IMAGE, added by task C2b.  /bin/[ is /bin/test: the same inode
# with a link count of 2, which is v7's arrangement and not a convenience -- cmd/test/test.c
# looks at argv[0] and requires a closing `]' when it was entered by this name, so without the
# link that branch of the program is unreachable code.  `link' takes a `target' and no
# `source' or `mode': there is nothing to copy in and nothing to set, the inode being the
# other entry's already (cmd/fsutil/manifest.cpp resolves links last, so the order here does
# not matter).  kernel/test's rootimg_link asserts the two share an inode once the image is
# built, for the same reason rootimg_setuid exists: nothing but the finished image can say.
link /bin/[
target /bin/test

# ---- THE SETUID PROGRAMS HERE: three that change a DIRECTORY, and since task C6 --
# ---- three that change WHO YOU ARE ------------------------------------------------
#
# `mode 04755' -- 04000 is ISUID, and it is the whole of the work.  b6fsutil carries it
# through: manifest.cpp parses the number as an octal literal and add_file() writes
# `IFREG | (mode & 07777)' into the inode, and 07777 includes 04000.  The kernel end is
# getxfile()'s SUID block in kernel/sys1.c.
#
# WHY THEY NEED IT.  There is no mkdir(2), rmdir(2) or rename(2) on this system; a directory
# is built, taken apart and re-parented by hand, and every call that does so is gated on
# suser() -- mknod(), the directory arm of link(), and the directory arm of unlink().  None of
# the three programs grants more than that: each makes its own access() check against the REAL
# uid on the parent directory first, so root is borrowed for those calls and for nothing else.
# cmd/mkdir/README.md is the general account and cmd/mv/README.md is mv's.
#
# mv is the one to look at twice, because it is the only one that is setuid for SOME of what
# it does: mv.c returns into mvdir() before it reaches setuid(getuid()), so a directory rename
# keeps the borrowed root and every other form of mv drops it first.  Moving that one line
# would hand root to a plain file move.
#
# NOTHING IN build/rootfs/ CARRIES A MODE -- the staged file is an ordinary 0755 build
# artifact and this line is the only place the bit exists.  Which is why rootimg_setuid
# (kernel/test/CMakeLists.txt) reads it back off the finished image rather than trusting it.

file /bin/mkdir         # cmd/mkdir -- SETUID ROOT: mknod(2) and link(2) on a directory
source ../../rootfs/bin/mkdir
mode 04755

file /bin/mv            # cmd/mv -- SETUID ROOT: link(2) and unlink(2) on a directory
source ../../rootfs/bin/mv
mode 04755

file /bin/rmdir         # cmd/rmdir -- SETUID ROOT: unlink(2) on a directory
source ../../rootfs/bin/rmdir
mode 04755

# THE OTHER THREE ARE TASK C6's, and what they borrow root for is not a directory but an
# IDENTITY.  cmd/login is the program that made them possible and it is deliberately NOT
# setuid -- its privilege is inherited from getty, which init forked as root -- so until
# these three there was no way to change uid or gid from a shell at all.  cmd/login/README.md
# named su in advance for exactly this reason.
#
# Each drops what it borrowed, and the ORDER is load-bearing in the same way login's is:
# setuid(2) moves the REAL uid as well (kernel/sys4.c), so there is no way back afterwards
# and it has to be the last privileged act each of them performs.
#
#   su       setuid()/setgid() to the account that answered the prompt.  getuid() is the
#            real uid, which is what makes v7's `getuid() == 0 skips the password' correct.
#   newgrp   setgid() to a group the caller is not in -- allowed only through suser() --
#            and then setuid(getuid()) straight back down.
#   passwd   the narrowest of the three: it wants nothing but write permission on this
#            file, which is 0644 and root's.
#
# THEY ARE ALSO WHAT FINALLY MAKES getxfile()'s ISUID BRANCH ORDINARY.  cmd/README.md §8
# records that the branch is taken only `if (u.u_uid != 0)' and that every shell on this
# image used to be root's, so lib/test/suidt had to drop to uid 7 on purpose to reach it.
# A guest who types `su' reaches it by accident now, which is the point.

file /bin/newgrp        # cmd/newgrp -- SETUID ROOT: setgid(2) to a group you are not in
source ../../rootfs/bin/newgrp
mode 04755

file /bin/passwd        # cmd/passwd -- SETUID ROOT: it writes /etc/passwd, which is root's
source ../../rootfs/bin/passwd
mode 04755

file /bin/su            # cmd/su -- SETUID ROOT: setuid(2)/setgid(2) to another account
source ../../rootfs/bin/su
mode 04755

# ---- the static files of /etc ----------------------------------------------------
#
# /etc/rc is 0644, NOT 0755: runcom() in cmd/init/init.c execs `sh /etc/rc', never the
# script itself.  There is no `#!' on this system -- the shebang is a 4.xBSD invention, and
# neither getxfile() nor b6sim implements it -- so a script is never executed directly;
# either the shell is told to read it, or exec comes back ENOEXEC and the shell reads it
# anyway (cmd/sh/README.md).

file /etc/rc            # the boot script; etc/rc
source ../../rootfs/etc/rc
mode 0644

file /etc/motd          # the message of the day, printed by /etc/rc; etc/motd
source ../../rootfs/etc/motd
mode 0644

file /etc/passwd        # v7's, read by `ls -l' to turn a uid into a name; etc/passwd
source ../../rootfs/etc/passwd
mode 0644

file /etc/group         # the groups /etc/passwd's gids name; etc/group
source ../../rootfs/etc/group
mode 0644

# THE FILE THAT TURNS THIS INTO A MULTI-USER SYSTEM, and the whole of what task 29b had to
# add beyond two programs.  cmd/init/init.c's merge() reads it and forks an /etc/getty per
# enabled line; without it merge() returns at the failed open, multiple() falls straight
# through, and the boot is the single-user loop this port lived in until now.  Two lines,
# `console' and `tty1' -- the two Consuls, dev/sc.c's minors 0 and 1.  Format: ttys(5)
# (include/man/ttys.5) and rline() in cmd/init/init.c.  It is also what makes ttyslot(3)
# answer, and therefore getlogin(3): lib/test/ttyt is the assertion of that.
file /etc/ttys          # the terminal lines a getty sits on; etc/ttys
source ../../rootfs/etc/ttys
mode 0644

# The terminal capability database, read by tgetent() in lib/libtermcap.  BSD's
# termcap.small verbatim -- five entries, no `dumb', and vt100's `if=' names a tabset file
# that is not on this image; lib/libtermcap/README.md says why none of that is edited out.
# /usr/test/termcapt reads it from here, and reads THE SAME FILE (etc/termcap in the source
# tree) under b6sim through $TERMCAP.
file /etc/termcap       # the terminal capability database; etc/termcap
source ../../rootfs/etc/termcap
mode 0644

# ---- the library test programs, task 25c ------------------------------------------
#
# lib/test/*.c, the same twenty-four images b6sim runs (lib/test/CMakeLists.txt), staged
# into build/rootfs/usr/test/ by b6_libtest() and run HERE by the booted kernel --
# kernel/test/libtest.sh drives them, kernel/test/run-libtest.sh diffs what they wrote.
# Under b6sim every syscall is the host's; this is where sysent[] meets a real caller.
#
# THE LIST IS ALSO IN lib/test/progs.cmake, which is what kernel/test/CMakeLists.txt reads
# for ROOTFS_FILES and for the per-program ctest cases.  A manifest is a static file and
# can be generated from nothing, so these stanzas are the one copy kept by hand; adding a
# program means editing both.
#
# spawn is NOT here and shellt is: spawn's premise is that /bin/sh cannot be exec'd, which
# is true only under b6sim, and shellt is the other half of it -- see either source.  memt
# is here for the same reason and is not a libc test at all: it reads the kernel's own
# memory through /dev/kmem and its own image through /dev/mem, neither of which b6sim has.
# Nor is suidt: it drops to uid 7 and execs the two setuid programs above, which is the only
# way anything on this image reaches getxfile()'s ISUID branch.  It is 0755 itself -- the
# program that TESTS the bit must not carry it, or the drop it depends on would not happen.
#
# puret is the one PURE (NMAGIC) program among them, and one of only two images on this
# disk whose text the kernel can share at all -- /bin/sh is the other.  Task 26.

file /usr/lib/diffh     # cmd/diffh -- task C5f.  diff -h execv's this path; see the
                        # /usr/lib stanza above.  Its window is RANGE (30) lines of each
                        # file, so its storage is bounded by a constant where diff's is
                        # bounded by the input -- which is the whole reason -h exists.
source ../../rootfs/usr/lib/diffh
mode 0755

file /usr/dict/words     # cmd/look -- task C5b.  Sorted in BYTE order, which is the order look
                        # compares in: char is unsigned here, so every Cyrillic entry
                        # follows every ASCII one.  `LC_ALL=C sort -c words' is the check.
source ../../rootfs/usr/dict/words
mode 0644

file /usr/test/hello
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/hello
mode 0755

file /usr/test/vararg
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/vararg
mode 0755

file /usr/test/errno
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/errno
mode 0755

file /usr/test/procs
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/procs
mode 0755

file /usr/test/sbrkt
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/sbrkt
mode 0755

file /usr/test/malloct
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/malloct
mode 0755

file /usr/test/strings
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/strings
mode 0755

file /usr/test/gen
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/gen
mode 0755

file /usr/test/strtolt
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/strtolt
mode 0755

file /usr/test/environ
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/environ
mode 0755

file /usr/test/jmp
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/jmp
mode 0755

file /usr/test/headers
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/headers
mode 0755

file /usr/test/stdiot
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/stdiot
mode 0755

file /usr/test/printft
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/printft
mode 0755

file /usr/test/scanft
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/scanft
mode 0755

file /usr/test/execs
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/execs
mode 0755

file /usr/test/shellt
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/shellt
mode 0755

file /usr/test/memt
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/memt
mode 0755

file /usr/test/suidt     # 0755 and not 04755: see the note above
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/suidt
mode 0755

file /usr/test/timet
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/timet
mode 0755

file /usr/test/pwent
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/pwent
mode 0755

# ttyt is pwent's other half, and is here rather than in both worlds because /etc/ttys is:
# ttyname(0), ttyslot() and getlogin() answer for their failure paths under b6sim and for
# real here.  Task 29b.
file /usr/test/ttyt
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/ttyt
mode 0755

file /usr/test/signals
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/signals
mode 0755

file /usr/test/matht
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/matht
mode 0755

# termcapt is lib/libtermcap's, not libc's, and it is here for the reason memt is: the
# thing it reads -- /etc/termcap, above -- exists on this image and nowhere else.  Under
# b6sim it is handed the same file out of the source tree instead (lib/test/termcapt.args).
file /usr/test/termcapt
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/termcapt
mode 0755

# cursest and curstty are lib/libcurses', and they read /etc/termcap for termcapt's reason.
# cursest runs in both worlds: it sets My_term so that initscr() never calls gettmode(),
# which is what makes b6sim and this kernel agree -- b6sim's ioctl is a no-op and this
# console is ECHO|CRMOD|XTABS, so GT and NONL would otherwise differ and change the cursor
# motion curses emits.  curstty is the half that follows from that: it does NOT set My_term,
# and therefore runs HERE ONLY.  It is also the LAST program libtest.sh runs, because
# gettmode() clears XTABS on the real console and only endwin() puts it back.
file /usr/test/cursest
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/cursest
mode 0755

file /usr/test/curstty
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/curstty
mode 0755

# puret is the only PURE (NMAGIC) image on the disk, and the only reason kernel/text.c's
# xalloc()/xexpand()/xccdec() run at all: getxfile() forces ux_tsize to 0 for an FMAGIC
# image, so every other program here has no shared text to share.  kernel/test/swap runs
# three copies of it at once.

file /usr/test/puret
source ../../rootfs/usr/test/puret
mode 0755

# ---- character devices ----------------------------------------------------------

cdev /dev/console       # cdevsw[0] minor 0: the operator's console, dev/sc.c
major 0
minor 0
mode 0622

# The same device as /dev/console, under the name /etc/ttys and getty use.  A SEPARATE
# INODE, not a link, and that is deliberate: ttyname(3) (lib/libc/gen/ttyname.c) scans
# /dev matching the I-NUMBER, so each name reports itself.  One inode under two names
# would make the answer depend on directory order.
cdev /dev/tty0          # cdevsw[0] minor 0: Consul 1, again
major 0
minor 0
mode 0622

cdev /dev/tty1          # cdevsw[0] minor 1: Consul 2, the second terminal
major 0
minor 1
mode 0622

cdev /dev/mem           # cdevsw[1] minor 0: physical memory, dev/mem.c
major 1
minor 0
mode 0640

cdev /dev/kmem          # cdevsw[1] minor 1: kernel virtual memory
major 1
minor 1
mode 0640

cdev /dev/null          # cdevsw[1] minor 2: the bit bucket
major 1
minor 2
mode 0666

cdev /dev/tty           # cdevsw[2]: the controlling terminal, dev/sys.c
major 2
minor 0
mode 0666

cdev /dev/rmd0          # cdevsw[3]: the raw disk, dev/md.c
major 3
minor 0
mode 0600

cdev /dev/rmd1          # cdevsw[3] minor 1: the SECOND drive -- SIMH's md01
major 3
minor 1
mode 0600

cdev /dev/rmb0          # cdevsw[4]: the raw drum, dev/mb.c
major 4
minor 0
mode 0600

# ---- block devices --------------------------------------------------------------

bdev /dev/md0           # bdevsw[0]: the root disk -- rootdev = makedev(0, 0)
major 0
minor 0
mode 0600

bdev /dev/md1           # bdevsw[0] minor 1: the second drive, for mount(1M) -- task C4f
major 0
minor 1
mode 0600

# A THIRD DRIVE, AND IT IS HERE TO BE REFUSED.  kernel/test/mount attaches a blank pack on
# it -- formatted by SIMH's `attach -n', so every zone reads back as zeros -- and mounts it
# on purpose, because sbcheck() (kernel/alloc.c) answering EINVAL on a device with no
# filesystem is what retires cmd/mount/mount.1m's oldest BUGS line: v7's mount(2) believes
# whatever superblock it reads and crashes later.  There is no /dev/rmd2 beside it; nothing
# has ever wanted the raw form of a drive that holds nothing.
bdev /dev/md2           # bdevsw[0] minor 2: the third drive -- SIMH's md02
major 0
minor 2
mode 0600

# THE REST OF THE MOUNT TABLE.  NMOUNT is 8 and the root holds mount[0], so these five plus
# md1 and md2 are the seven drives that can be mounted at once.  Only md3 is attached by any
# test today -- kernel/test/mount hangs a second filesystem on it to prove that mount[2] can
# be filled at all -- and the other four are nodes for drives nobody has attached, which
# costs nothing: see the header above.
bdev /dev/md3           # bdevsw[0] minor 3: SIMH's md03 -- kernel/test/mount's second mount
major 0
minor 3
mode 0600

bdev /dev/md4           # bdevsw[0] minor 4: SIMH's md04
major 0
minor 4
mode 0600

bdev /dev/md5           # bdevsw[0] minor 5: SIMH's md05
major 0
minor 5
mode 0600

bdev /dev/md6           # bdevsw[0] minor 6: SIMH's md06
major 0
minor 6
mode 0600

bdev /dev/md7           # bdevsw[0] minor 7: SIMH's md07
major 0
minor 7
mode 0600

bdev /dev/swap          # bdevsw[1]: the drums -- swapdev = makedev(1, 0)
major 1
minor 0
mode 0600
